// `@meta(x: T).name` WHERE T IS A PRIMITIVE, which the three existing
// meta tests never ask. All three ask about a declaration the user
// wrote in the file -- a struct, an enum, an alias, a generic
// instantiation -- and a primitive is the one written type expression
// that looks like it has no declaration to read a name off at all.
//
// IT HAS ONE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `sema_meta.zen`'s `written_kind`
// sends EVERY `Named` spelling through `written_decl`, which looks the
// name up in the module's definitions -- and `std/core/num.zen` really
// does declare `i64* = { MIN*, MAX* }`, a companion struct that exists
// to carry the constants. So `@meta(x: i64).name` answers `i64`
// because a struct in num.zen happens to be spelled `i64`, and nothing
// in the tree checked that the two agree. The comment over
// `written_kind` says the opposite in as many words -- "any other
// spelling, `@Self`, a primitive, has no written declaration, so the
// RESOLVED type answers" -- and a change that made the code match its
// own comment refuses every line below, because `resolved_decl`
// refuses a type that is not `Named`.
//
// THE WIDTHS ARE THE ORACLE. Every name is printed WITH ITS `.len`, as
// `type_name_via_meta` established, and the five primitives here have
// four different lengths (3, 4, 2, 5). So a fold that answered with the
// wrong prim, or with a family name -- `int` for every signed width,
// `uint` for every unsigned one -- moves a length as well as a
// spelling, and neither `i64 3` nor `usize 5` is a shape a default
// produces. Routing primitives to the empty string, which is the
// plausible wrong answer design_meta.md 6.4 names, prints ` 0` five
// times. `str` and `bool` ride along because their companions are
// declared in other modules again (`std.text`, the prelude), so a
// lookup that only ever reached `std.core.num` still moves two lines.
//
// THE LAST LINE IS THE CONTROL, and it is what makes the five above
// mean something: `Wrap<Wrap<i64>>` folds to `Wrap` through the
// ordinary user-declaration route, so a mutation that breaks only the
// primitives leaves it standing and the diff names the half that
// moved.

Wrap<T> = {
    v: T,
}

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("{} {}", @meta(x: i64).name, @meta(x: i64).name.len);
    println("{} {}", @meta(x: bool).name, @meta(x: bool).name.len);
    println("{} {}", @meta(x: u8).name, @meta(x: u8).name.len);
    println("{} {}", @meta(x: usize).name, @meta(x: usize).name.len);
    println("{} {}", @meta(x: str).name, @meta(x: str).name.len);
    println(
        "{} {}",
        @meta(x: Wrap<Wrap<i64>>).name,
        @meta(x: Wrap<Wrap<i64>>).name.len
    );
    Ok(0);
}
